<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Praetorian Guard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/27-bce">27 BCE</a>–<a href="/years/312">312</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as cohortes praetoriae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Praetorian Guard was the imperial guard of the Roman army, serving the emperor as bodyguard, in crowd control and in gathering intelligence. Under the Republic praetorians guarded generals, first recorded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-family">Scipio family</a> around 275 BCE; in 27 BCE Augustus made them his personal escort. For three centuries they were known for palace intrigue, overthrowing emperors and proclaiming successors: their prefect Sejanus gathered great power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, and they once put the empire up to auction to Didius Julianus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> disbanded the guard in 312 and destroyed its barracks, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castrum">Castra</a> Praetoria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard.jpg" alt="Praetorian Guard" width="330" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sunappu-shashin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_historique_dit_Relief_des_Prétoriens_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>During the longer campaigns of the late Republic, a personal bodyguard was the norm for a commander in the field. In camp, a cohors praetoria stood near the praetorium, the commander's tent. Generals with imperium also held office and had lictors, but in the absence of a permanent bodyguard senior officers protected themselves with temporary units of selected soldiers.</p><p>At the end of 40 BCE two members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a>, Octavian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, both had praetorian guards. Octavian installed his within the pomerium, the religious and legal boundary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the first occasion on which troops were permanently garrisoned in the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Augustus, careful to keep a Republican veneer, allowed only nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, at first of 500 men each and later of 1,000, so that three could be on duty in the capital at once. In 2 BCE he appointed two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a>, Quintus Ostorius Scapula and Publius Salvius Aper. In 26 CE the prefect Sejanus gathered the cohorts, scattered through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, into one camp, the Castra Praetoria, beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servian-wall">Servian Wall</a>.</p><p>The guard fought on campaign as well as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>: against the mutinies of 14 CE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, in the Year of the Four Emperors, in wars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>. Politically it grew exceptionally ambitious after the death of Sejanus. In 41 conspirators from the Senate and the Guard killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, and the Praetorians installed his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. In 193, after killing Pertinax, they auctioned the empire to Didius Julianus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> then disbanded them and formed a new guard from his Pannonian legions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The prefect commanded the guard from 2 BCE, and from the time of Vespasian was always an equestrian. From the 2nd century the prefect also oversaw the rest of the garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After Constantine dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, the prefect's role became purely administrative, ruling large territories in the emperor's name.</p><p>Recruits were first drawn from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and at 15 to 32 years of age were older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> recruits. Service was shorter than in the legions, and pay was higher: pay-and-a-half, so that a legionary's 250 denarii became 375, to which each new emperor's donativum was added. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> raised the guard's pay to 1,500 denarii a year. To avoid alienating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s population, the Praetorians wore a toga rather than armour in the heart of the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Praetorian Guard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 193 CE Rome’s <b>Praetorian Guard</b> murdered the emperor Pertinax and sold the throne to Didius Julianus, which is why political scientists speak of “praetorianism” when soldiers make and unmake governments.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Equestrian prefects governed Egypt and commanded the <b>Praetorian Guard</b>, procurators ran the imperial estates and taxes, and equestrian officers commanded auxiliary units.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</a></li><li><blockquote>Abandoned by the Senate and the <b>Praetorian Guard</b>, Didius Julianus was killed in the palace after a reign of 66 days.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-1">1 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Praetorian Guard</b> killed the teenage emperor Elagabalus, whose religious innovations had caused scandal, together with his mother, Julia Soaemias.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-13">13 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After killing Emperor Pertinax, the <b>Praetorian Guard</b> sold the throne to the senator Didius Julianus, who outbid his rival.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-28">28 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
